        passageEn:
            "On the Monday before the inter-schools debate final, Htet Aung noticed a thin crack creeping across the glass of the trophy case in the main hall, right beside last year's winning shield. He mentioned it to no one — it seemed such a small, unimportant thing compared to the speech he still needed to finish writing. Outside, the sky had turned an odd, heavy grey, though the forecast had promised sunshine all week.\n\nBy Wednesday, the crack had spread a little further, and Htet Aung's team captain, U Zaw, pulled him aside with an unusually serious look. 'Something doesn't feel right about this final,' U Zaw murmured, glancing at the darkening windows. 'Just… be ready for anything.' Htet Aung laughed it off, certain his teammate was simply nervous, and went back to rehearsing his closing argument one more time.\n\nOn the morning of the final itself, a sudden thunderstorm knocked out the school's electricity ten minutes before the debate was due to begin, plunging the hall into shadow exactly where the cracked glass case stood. Htet Aung remembered the crack, remembered the grey sky, remembered U Zaw's warning — and realised, standing there in the half-dark hall, that the story had been quietly warning him all along. He took a slow breath, picked up his notes by memory alone, and stepped forward to speak anyway.",
