        passageEn:
            "Htet Aung stood at the same assembly microphone where, months earlier, he had sung his ballad about his grandmother's lantern until half the school joined the refrain. This time his hands were steady rather than shaking, and the paper in front of him held no rhymes at all — only his finished report-and-explanation feature on Myanmar's rainforests, ready for the school's environmental assembly.\n\nHe began with the report section, reading his classification subheadings aloud — Location and Climate, Wildlife, Threats — in a calm, impersonal voice, generalisers doing the quiet work: 'Most rainforest mammals are nocturnal. Deforestation threatens habitats across the region.' Then he moved into the explanation section, his tone shifting only slightly as the connectives took over: 'Because the canopy blocks sunlight, the forest floor stays cool and damp. As a result, delicate plants can survive there that could not survive anywhere else.' A labelled diagram of the canopy sat behind him on the screen, exactly as Ms. Nandar had taught.\n\nWhen he finished, the headteacher told the gathered teachers and parents that the school's Year 6 writers had grown remarkably this year — from ballads sung with trembling voices to careful, evidence-based reports that could stand entirely on their own. Htet Aung smiled, thinking of the forest that had once frightened him, then inspired his poem, and now, in this final and most formal shape of all, had taught the whole assembly something true.",
