        passageEn:
            "Nay Chi's class was asked to count and sort every toy in the classroom toy box for a tidy-up project. \"I found three cars and two balls,\" Nay Chi called out, placing them into neat piles. Zaw Zaw added, \"I found five books and two boxes of crayons.\"\n\nMoe Moe pulled out a small bag and gasped. \"There are six mice in here -- toy mice, not real ones!\" she laughed, holding up the tiny plastic figures. \"That's a tricky plural word,\" their teacher said with a smile. \"Mouse becomes mice, not mouses.\"\n\nAs they kept sorting, Zaw Zaw found a set of plastic feet from an old puzzle. \"Look, these are feet from the puzzle person!\" he said. \"Foot becomes feet, remember?\" Nay Chi reminded him, remembering their lesson from earlier that week.\n\nBy the end of the tidy-up, they had counted everything: cars, balls, books, boxes, mice, and feet. \"We used both regular and irregular plurals today,\" their teacher said proudly. \"Great counting, everyone!\" The three friends looked at their neatly organized toy box, happy with a job well done.",
