        passageEn:
            "Nay Chi felt nervous about performing her poem in front of the whole class. \"What if I forget the words?\" she worried, gripping her poem tightly in her hands.\n\nMoe Moe offered to help her practice. \"Let's try reading it together first,\" she suggested. As Nay Chi read the exciting lines about a thunderstorm, Moe Moe encouraged her to raise her voice. \"Louder here, like real thunder!\" she said, and Nay Chi's voice grew bolder.\n\nZaw Zaw joined in too, reminding Nay Chi to pause after each line. \"Give the class time to picture it,\" he explained, showing her how a short pause made the poem feel more powerful. Nay Chi practiced her facial expressions in a mirror, widening her eyes for the scary parts of the storm.\n\nBy the time the performance day arrived, Nay Chi felt ready. She read her poem with confidence, raising her voice for the thunder and pausing at just the right moments. Her classmates clapped loudly when she finished. \"That was amazing!\" Moe Moe cheered. Nay Chi smiled, grateful that practice had truly made her performance perfect.",
