            "For the term's final challenge, their teacher gave Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw a short story to analyze using every skill they had learned. The story read: \"First, dark clouds covered the sky. Next, Nay Chi's little brother refused to go outside, hiding under a blanket. Finally, thunder rumbled loudly in the distance.\"\n\n\"I can sequence this easily,\" Moe Moe said. \"First the clouds, next the hiding, finally the thunder.\" She wrote the events in order on her worksheet, feeling confident about this skill.\n\nNay Chi tackled the inference question next. \"The brother hid under a blanket because he was probably scared of the coming storm,\" she explained, \"even though the story never says the word 'scared' directly.\"\n\nZaw Zaw took on the fact and opinion question. \"'Thunder rumbled in the distance' is a fact, because it's something that happened and can be described,\" he said. \"But if someone said 'thunderstorms are the scariest weather,' that would be an opinion, since not everyone would agree.\" Their teacher checked their work with a proud smile. \"You've used sequencing, inference, and fact versus opinion perfectly,\" she said. \"You are truly ready for anything you read next.\"",
