      kind: LessonKind.reading,
      xpReward: 10,
      readingPassage: ReadingPassageModel(
        titleEn: "Zayar's Timed Practice",
        titleMy: "ဇေယျာ၏ အချိန်ကန့်သတ်လေ့ကျင့်ခန်း",
        passageEn:
            "Zayar's Paper 2 revision session gave him 60 minutes to complete both a directed writing task and a composition task. In his first attempt, he spent 45 minutes carefully perfecting his directed writing letter, revising word choices repeatedly, and had only 15 minutes left for the entire composition -- far too little time to plan and write a complete narrative piece.\n\nHis teacher pointed out that this reflected poor time allocation, not poor writing skill. Zayar had produced an excellent directed writing piece, but his rushed, underdeveloped composition dragged his overall performance down significantly. The lesson wasn't that he needed to write better -- it was that he needed to manage his available time more evenly across both required tasks.\n\nFor his second attempt, Zayar planned his time explicitly before writing anything: five minutes to read and plan the directed writing task, twenty minutes to write it, five minutes to plan the composition, and the remaining twenty-five minutes to write and briefly review the composition, plus five minutes at the very end for a final check across both pieces. He set an informal mental checkpoint at the 30-minute mark to confirm he was on track to move to the composition task on schedule.\n\nWhen the 30-minute checkpoint arrived, Zayar noticed he was running two minutes behind on the directed writing task. Rather than ignoring this and continuing to perfect it, he made a deliberate choice to wrap up his current point and transition to the composition slightly earlier than planned, accepting a marginally less polished directed writing piece in exchange for a properly developed composition. This adjustment meant both pieces received adequate attention rather than one being excellent and the other rushed.\n\nHis teacher's feedback praised this shift specifically: \"Your writing skill was never the problem. Today you practiced the skill that actually determines your overall Paper 2 score: managing two tasks fairly, rather than pouring all your care into just one.\"",
        passageMy:
